module takes three inputs, a, b, and c, and produces an output out after three clock cycles. The circuit uses three stages of registers (stage0, stage1, stage2) to store intermediate results, each 36 bits wide to accommodate the multiplication of two 18-bit numbers. The first stage computes the product of a and b and subtracts c from it. The result is then passed through two additional stages without modification. The final output is the result after three clock cycles.
The key input signals are a, b, c, and the clock signal clk, which controls the flow of data through the pipeline. The output signal out provides the computed result.
The design operates sequentially, with each stage updating on the rising edge of the clock, and does not include any combinational logic beyond the initial multiplication and subtraction.
`timescale 1ns / 1ps
module mulsub_3_stage_signed_18_bit(
	input signed [17:0] a,
	input signed [17:0] b,
	input signed [17:0] c,
	output [17:0] out,
	input clk);
reg [35:0] stage0;
reg [35:0] stage1;
reg [35:0] stage2;
assign out = stage2[35:18];
always @(posedge clk) begin
	stage0 <= a * b - c;
	stage1 <= stage0;
	stage2 <= stage1;
end
endmodule

