module, `stratixiv_pciehip_pciexp_dcram_rtry`, which is used for implementing a dual-clock RAM with separate write and read ports. The module uses parameters to define the address and data bus widths, which are set to 4 bits and 32 bits by default. It then describes a RAM array of size 16, with each element holding 32 bits of data. 
The write operation is controlled by the `wrclock` signal, capturing the `wren`, `wraddress`, and `data` inputs into registers (`wren_r`, `wraddr_r`, and `wrdata_r`) on each rising edge. If `wren_r` is high, data is written to `ram_block` at the specified address.
The read operation, triggered by `rdclock`, captures the `rdaddress` into `read_addr_r`, and the output `q` is continuously assigned from `ram_block` based on this read address, making it combinational.
This design is suitable for dual-clock systems, though it is missing reset logic and may require external synchronization to prevent data conflicts.
`timescale 1 ns/100 ps
module stratixiv_pciehip_pciexp_dcram_rtry (
   wrclock,
   wren,
   wraddress,
   data,
   rdclock,
   rdaddress,
   q
   );
parameter addr_width = 4;
parameter data_width = 32;
input   wrclock; 
input   wren; 
input   [data_width - 1:0] data; 
input   [addr_width - 1:0] wraddress; 
input	rdclock;
input   [addr_width - 1:0] rdaddress; 
output  [data_width - 1:0] q;
reg  [data_width - 1:0] ram_block [0:15];
reg  [addr_width - 1:0] wraddr_r;
reg  [data_width - 1:0] wrdata_r;
reg  wren_r;
reg  [addr_width - 1:0] read_addr_r;
always @(posedge wrclock)
begin
   wren_r <= wren;
   wraddr_r <= wraddress;
   wrdata_r <= data;
   if (wren_r == 1'b1)
      ram_block[wraddr_r] <= wrdata_r;
end
assign q = ram_block[read_addr_r];
always @(posedge rdclock)
begin
   read_addr_r <= rdaddress;
end
endmodule

