module using a non-restoring algorithm. The module takes two 16-bit inputs, A and B, and produces a 16-bit output, Res, which represents the quotient of the division. The algorithm performs division through repeated subtraction, iterating up to 16 times to compute the result. Key registers include a1 and b1, which store the current values of A and B during each iteration, and p1, a 17-bit register used to track the partial result. The always block triggers on changes to A or B, using a for loop to shift and subtract values, determining each bit of the quotient sequentially. The final output Res is assigned the value of a1 after completing the loop. This design efficiently handles division without restoring previous states, suitable for hardware implementation.
    `define WIDTH 16
    `define N 15
    reg [`WIDTH-1:0] a1,b1;
    reg [`WIDTH:0] p1;
    integer i;
    always @(A or B) begin
        a1 = A;
        b1 = B;
        p1 = 0;
        for(i=0;i<=`WIDTH;i=i+1)begin
            p1 = {p1[`WIDTH-2:0],a1[`WIDTH-1]};
            a1[`WIDTH-1:1] = a1[`WIDTH-2:0];
            p1 = p1-b1;
            if(p1[`WIDTH-1] == 1)begin
                a1[0] = 0;
                p1 = p1 + b1;
            end
            else
                a1[0] = 1;
        end
    Res = a1;
    end
endmodule

